    "## 4. Multi-Stage Automated Data Extraction Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "**Pipeline Architecture:**\n",
    "```text\n",
    "[ Unstructured PDFs ]\n",
    "       |\n",
    "       v\n",
    "[ MuPDF (Raw Perfect Text) ]  +  [ GROBID (XML Structural Tags) ]\n",
    "       |                              |\n",
    "       +--------------+---------------+\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Fuzzy String Reconciliation & State Machine ]\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Stage 1: Structurally Tagged JSON (Abstract, Methods, Results, etc.) ]\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Stage 2: Metadata Injection (Merge GROBID Titles/DOIs with MuPDF Content) ]\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Text Preprocessing & Token Optimization ]\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Stage 3: AWS Bedrock Batch Inference (Claude Sonnet) ]\n",
    "                      |\n",
    "                      v\n",
    "[ Export & Enrichment to Relational CSVs ]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stage 1: Hybrid Text Extraction & Structural Reconciliation\n",
    "* Converting visually complex PDFs into machine-readable formats without losing characters requires a dual-engine approach.\n",
    "* **MuPDF & GROBID Integration:** The system uses the MuPDF C engine to extract perfect raw text strings while simultaneously sending the PDF to a local GROBID server to map the XML document structure (headers, abstracts, body paragraphs).\n",
    "* **Fuzzy Reconciliation Engine:** A custom Python pipeline runs a two-tier state machine that aligns the perfect MuPDF text blocks with the GROBID XML tags using `difflib` sequence matching.\n",
    "* **Heuristic Fallback:** If GROBID misses a block of text, a regex-driven fallback intelligently classifies the orphaned text based on length, prefix numbering and keywords.\n",
    "* **Smoothing & Merging:** Consecutive text blocks sharing the same section label are merged into clean continuous JSON blobs. This automatically filters out publisher artifacts and page numbers.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stage 2: Metadata Injection & Deduplication\n",
    "* To ensure downstream citation matching remains perfectly accurate, the pipeline isolates and merges the best components from both extraction engines.\n",
    "* **Precision Metadata Harvesting:** While MuPDF yields superior full-text extraction, GROBID is optimized for academic header metadata. A custom Python tool extracts the title, doi and authors exclusively from the GROBID-processed files.\n",
    "* **Master JSON Compilation:** The tool uses a pre-filtered list of deduplicated files as a master reference and dynamically maps the precise GROBID metadata to the flawless MuPDF body content. This outputs a finalized master directory of JSONs ready for LLM ingestion.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stage 3: Deep Biochemical Feature Extraction via AWS Bedrock\n",
    "* The final data extraction is handled by an automated AWS Bedrock pipeline running Claude Sonnet. The script packages the preprocessed JSONs into JSONL format, uploads them to an S3 bucket and triggers asynchronous batch inference to bypass API throttling.\n",
    "* The system prompt enforces strict relational logic:\n",
    "  * **The Degradation Short-Circuit:** The model first determines if the paper actually tests enzymes for the degradation of target polymers. If the paper discusses general medical enzymes or the biosynthesis/accumulation of bioplastics, it short-circuits the extraction and outputs a negative flag to save compute time.\n",
    "  * **Absolute Relational Accuracy:** The prompt forces the LLM to map entities using strictly generated primary and foreign keys (e.g. EXP_1, PROT_1, VAR_1, ASSAY_1). It ensures that multiple enzyme variants tested across varying environmental conditions are never mixed.\n",
    "  * **Data Consolidation:** Once the batch job completes, a Python handler downloads the S3 output, saves individual JSON records and parses the nested arrays to automatically append rows to six master CSV files."
   ]
